# Line endings for this repository.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Every text file in the index is LF and always has been --
# `git ls-files --eol` reports i/lf for all 150 of them. Nothing is wrong with
# the history. What happened is that an editor on Windows rewrote 73 working-
# tree files as CRLF, and with no attributes and core.autocrlf unset there was
# nothing to tell git those endings were not wanted. The result was a 2080-line
# diff across the NPU RTL, the firmware image and four testbenches in which not
# one byte of content had changed.
#
# That is the cost this file prevents: a real change -- a moved MAX_N, a fixed
# CSR bound -- landing as four lines inside two thousand, where no reviewer will
# see it.
#
# Attributes rather than core.autocrlf on purpose. autocrlf is per-clone config
# that every contributor has to set for themselves and that travels with nobody;
# this file travels with the repository and wins over autocrlf wherever the two
# disagree.

# Everything git decides is text is LF in the index AND LF on checkout, on every
# platform. `text=auto` is the detection; `eol=lf` is what makes a Windows
# checkout write LF rather than CRLF, which is the half that was missing.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- files where CRLF is not cosmetic ---------------------------------------
#
# A shell script with CRLF fails as `\r: command not found`; there are 9 of
# them here. The Yosys and linker scripts are the same shape of problem, and
# tb_c930_soc.sv loads the firmware image through $readmemh, which is only
# whitespace-tolerant by convention rather than by the standard. Named
# explicitly so they stay LF even if the auto-detection is ever narrowed.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.ys   text eol=lf
*.tcl  text eol=lf
*.ld   text eol=lf
*.S    text eol=lf
*.hex  text eol=lf

# --- source ------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sv   text eol=lf
*.v    text eol=lf
*.c    text eol=lf
*.cc   text eol=lf
*.h    text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.xdc  text eol=lf
*.lpf  text eol=lf
*.abc  text eol=lf

# --- binary: never touched, never diffed as text -----------------------------
#
# git already detects these four (i/-text). Declaring them means the detection
# is not what stands between a firmware image and a corrupting rewrite.
*.bin  binary
*.elf  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.pdf  binary

# --- tool droppings that are neither source nor worth diffing ----------------
#
# Vivado writes these into the working tree; -text keeps git from deciding they
# are text and rewriting them, without claiming they are meaningful binaries.
*.jou  -text
*.log  -text
*.lic  -text
